ns unit * 2\n  ---\n  duration_ms: 2.89\n  type: 'test'\n  ...\n1..1\n# tests 1\n# pass 1\n# fail 0\n```\n\n**The file:**\n\n```ts\nimport assert from 'node:assert/strict';\nimport { existsSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { registerHooks } from 'node:module';\nimport { test } from 'node:test';\n\n// src/double.ts imports the extensionless specifier './unit', which Node's ESM\n// resolver cannot resolve at runtime. Register a small resolve hook scoped to\n// this test that maps extensionless relative imports to their .ts file, so the\n// sources can be loaded unmodified (no build step, config, or new runner).\nregisterHooks({ ... resolve './x' -> './x.ts' ... });\n\ntest('double() returns unit * 2', async () => {\n  const { double } = await import('../src/double.ts');\n  assert.equal(double(), 42);\n  console.log('LONG_SESSION_OK');\n});\n```\n\n**Why the hook:** `package.json` declares `\"type\": \"module\"` but there's no TS config/bundler, so plain Node ESM fails on the extensionless `'./unit'` (`ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`, which I verified first). Since I must not add configuration, replace the test runner, or alter your `src/` files, the test itself registers a Node built-in `registerHooks` resolve hook to map that specifier to `unit.ts`. This keeps `src/double.ts` exactly as you asked and uses only native Node APIs (`node:test`, `node:assert`, `node:module`).\n\n**Nothing else was touched:** the only files in the workspace are `AGENTS.md`, `package.json` (both byte-identical to before — hashes verified), `src/unit.ts`, `src/double.ts`, and the new `test/long.test.ts`. I created the `test/` directory for it; no `oracle.test.ts` existed to preserve.\n\nIf you'd rather avoid the hook, the alternative is changing `src/double.ts` to import `'./unit.ts'` (or `'./unit.js'` with a build step) — one line, and I'll adjust the test accordingly.\nTest passes — no repair needed, so I made no changes.\n\n**Re-run result** (`node --test test/long.test.ts`):\n\n```\nTAP version 13\n#